<v David Boskovic>How do you get 2% better year over year, 5% if you're lucky?

386
00:24:54.820 --> 00:24:59.180
<v David Boskovic>How do you just iterate on this previous thing slowly and improve?

387
00:24:59.480 --> 00:25:03.340
<v David Boskovic>And even the advice on doing radical things is not that radical.

388
00:25:03.860 --> 00:25:08.700
<v David Boskovic>And if you look for mentors, advisors, and people who've done this before,

389
00:25:08.960 --> 00:25:10.880
<v David Boskovic>you actually are limited in who you find.

390
00:25:11.000 --> 00:25:14.200
<v David Boskovic>You might find other peers in my world, which I look up to.

391
00:25:14.300 --> 00:25:18.640
<v David Boskovic>But is there someone who's just been able to build an incredibly large,

392
00:25:18.640 --> 00:25:23.080
<v David Boskovic>large, successful outcome and done so and maintained the ability to have step function

393
00:25:23.080 --> 00:25:24.480
<v David Boskovic>advantages at that scale.

394
00:25:24.620 --> 00:25:27.320
<v David Boskovic>I think there's only one person who's done this, and he's relatively controversial.

395
00:25:27.520 --> 00:25:32.100
<v David Boskovic>I know who you, I'm sure you know who I'm going to say, but Elon Musk is the only person

396
00:25:32.100 --> 00:25:35.640
<v David Boskovic>who has been able to continue producing step functions at scale.

397
00:25:35.900 --> 00:25:39.440
<v David Boskovic>That is worth noting in the world of business, right?

398
00:25:39.520 --> 00:25:43.720
<v David Boskovic>Most of the time, step functions happen early in startups and then eventually start tapering

399
00:25:43.720 --> 00:25:47.640
<v David Boskovic>off as company reaches a billion dollars or whatever in valuation, right?

400
00:25:48.640 --> 00:25:50.820
<v David Boskovic>Start just like optimizing for what you've already learned.

401
00:25:51.020 --> 00:25:57.140
<v David Boskovic>Very few people have been able to continue to go and create the step functions after that stage.

402
00:25:57.320 --> 00:26:03.740
<v David Boskovic>And as such, I think there's a lot to learn from the approaches in building companies and businesses that Musk has taken.

403
00:26:05.180 --> 00:26:08.920
<v Noah Labhart>So with Flatfile, if you could go back to the beginning, what would you do different?

404
00:26:09.000 --> 00:26:10.820
<v Noah Labhart>Where would you consider taking a different approach?

405
00:26:11.020 --> 00:26:12.580
<v Noah Labhart>Does that have to be something that didn't work?

406
00:26:12.680 --> 00:26:15.820
<v Noah Labhart>Could have even worked pretty well, but maybe tweak it a little bit.

407
00:26:16.620 --> 00:26:22.360
<v David Boskovic>One of the things I said early on was, if this is ever going to become a company, I'm going to approach it like an investor.

408
00:26:22.500 --> 00:26:27.680
<v David Boskovic>I'm going to be the hardest to please person about whether or not this is going to be a company.

409
00:26:28.100 --> 00:26:36.220
<v David Boskovic>We did a lot of tests before we started investing any time in it that looked like getting a lot of customers involved.

410
00:26:36.360 --> 00:26:43.240
<v David Boskovic>That looked like digging deep into everything that customers told us that could be interpreted as, oh, this is exciting.

411
00:26:43.380 --> 00:26:44.740
<v David Boskovic>This is somewhere we can go with it.

412
00:26:45.800 --> 00:26:52.260
<v David Boskovic>Ultimately, we, after 18 months, eventually decided to build it out into a company and raise money and go with it.

413
00:26:52.400 --> 00:26:59.200
<v David Boskovic>That was largely because I didn't want to waste a lot of time on an idea that wasn't going to play out.

414
00:26:59.440 --> 00:27:03.920
<v David Boskovic>I think if I were to go back, knowing what I know now, I wouldn't have waited 18 months.

415
00:27:04.100 --> 00:27:05.380
<v David Boskovic>I would have dumped on it out of the gate.

416
00:27:06.060 --> 00:27:08.140
<v David Boskovic>I wouldn't have been quite so fearsome.

417
00:27:08.140 --> 00:27:10.700
<v David Boskovic>But carefulness isn't always bad early on.

418
00:27:10.800 --> 00:27:14.740
<v David Boskovic>And that allowed us to learn a lot before and throw ourselves into it.

419
00:27:14.760 --> 00:27:16.320
<v David Boskovic>And it allowed us to raise a lot of capital quickly.

420
00:27:16.740 --> 00:27:17.700
<v David Boskovic>So it's not all bad.

421
00:27:17.860 --> 00:27:20.280
<v David Boskovic>But certainly knowing what I know, I would have started earlier.

422
00:27:21.120 --> 00:27:22.140
<v Noah Labhart>David, last question.

423
00:27:22.300 --> 00:27:26.340
<v Noah Labhart>So you're getting on a plane and you're sitting next to a young entrepreneur who's built the next big thing.

424
00:27:26.600 --> 00:27:27.840
<v Noah Labhart>They're jazzed about it.

425
00:27:27.920 --> 00:27:30.900
<v Noah Labhart>They can't wait to show it off to the world and can't wait to show it off to you right there on the plane.

426
00:27:31.280 --> 00:27:35.040
<v Noah Labhart>What advice do you give that person having gone down this road a bit several times?

427
00:27:36.580 --> 00:27:41.840
<v David Boskovic>The first thing is just everything you do is going to be wrong from a product perspective.

428
00:27:42.420 --> 00:27:44.380
<v David Boskovic>Even when you think it's right, it's going to be wrong.

429
00:27:45.380 --> 00:27:46.880
<v David Boskovic>Assume that in everything you do.

430
00:27:46.920 --> 00:27:55.520
<v David Boskovic>If you assume that the first pass you're going to do is going to be wrong, you can stop wasting time researching it and trying to build the perfect product plan, perfect roadmap.

431
00:27:56.160 --> 00:28:04.080
<v David Boskovic>There's something really key about the humility it takes to sometimes develop through a lot of pain that it takes to understand this.

432
00:28:05.040 --> 00:28:12.640
<v David Boskovic>Because once you know that there's no amount of preparation that's going to make it right, perfectly right the first time, you can get in there and get started experimenting.

433
00:28:12.840 --> 00:28:14.800
<v David Boskovic>And things start moving so much faster when you do.

434
00:28:15.020 --> 00:28:22.560
<v David Boskovic>And I think the only times I've ever regretted the time I've spent on things is when I thought I could get it right on the first try.

435
00:28:22.740 --> 00:28:25.340
<v David Boskovic>And that just leads to very costly mistakes.

436
00:28:26.180 --> 00:28:28.780
<v David Boskovic>So assume you're going to get it wrong and get it out there.

437
00:28:28.860 --> 00:28:29.360
<v David Boskovic>Experiment.

438
00:28:29.420 --> 00:28:30.080
<v David Boskovic>Hear the feedback.

439
00:28:30.220 --> 00:28:30.920
<v David Boskovic>Hear how it's wrong.

440
00:28:31.120 --> 00:28:31.860
<v David Boskovic>Maybe you got close.

441
00:28:32.140 --> 00:28:33.520
<v David Boskovic>Maybe you got within 70%.

442
00:28:35.040 --> 00:28:35.760
<v David Boskovic>and getting closer.

443
00:28:35.900 --> 00:28:37.000
<v David Boskovic>Maybe you're so far off

444
00:28:37.000 --> 00:28:38.280
<v David Boskovic>that you don't get the reactions

445
00:28:38.280 --> 00:28:39.580
<v David Boskovic>that you need or the feedback you need

446
00:28:39.580 --> 00:28:40.580
<v David Boskovic>and you go back to the drawing board.

447
00:28:40.820 --> 00:28:42.620
<v David Boskovic>But just assume you're wrong.

448
00:28:43.340 --> 00:28:44.900
<v David Boskovic>The second piece is

449
00:28:44.900 --> 00:28:47.140
<v David Boskovic>the exact opposite sentiment.

450
00:28:48.020 --> 00:28:49.880
<v David Boskovic>And it's almost always,

451
00:28:50.240 --> 00:28:51.300
<v David Boskovic>I find that,

452
00:28:51.860 --> 00:28:53.260
<v David Boskovic>especially with first-time founders,

453
00:28:53.880 --> 00:28:55.060
<v David Boskovic>you get into it

454
00:28:55.060 --> 00:28:55.980
<v David Boskovic>and you start doing business

455
00:28:55.980 --> 00:28:57.040
<v David Boskovic>and you might run into

456
00:28:57.040 --> 00:28:58.280
<v David Boskovic>negotiating your first deal

457
00:28:58.280 --> 00:29:00.560
<v David Boskovic>or having to let go of your first exec.

458
00:29:00.960 --> 00:29:03.920
<v David Boskovic>And you second-guess everything, right?

459
00:29:03.980 --> 00:29:04.700
<v David Boskovic>You're like, oh man,

460
00:29:05.040 --> 00:29:06.560
<v David Boskovic>What if I'm the bad CEO here?

461
00:29:06.840 --> 00:29:08.340
<v David Boskovic>And what if this exec is great?

462
00:29:08.420 --> 00:29:10.400
<v David Boskovic>I'm just horrible at managing execs.

463
00:29:10.480 --> 00:29:13.860
<v David Boskovic>Or like, how come I can't get this deal over the line?

464
00:29:13.920 --> 00:29:16.780
<v David Boskovic>Why is it 30% less than I thought it was going to be?

465
00:29:16.900 --> 00:29:18.640
<v David Boskovic>And I feel like I should be a better negotiator.

466
00:29:18.680 --> 00:29:19.500
<v David Boskovic>And there's just constant.

467
00:29:19.660 --> 00:29:21.440
<v David Boskovic>And yes, you probably are right.

468
00:29:21.600 --> 00:29:22.920
<v David Boskovic>You will get that deal done.

469
00:29:23.160 --> 00:29:24.000
<v David Boskovic>You will move on.

470
00:29:24.040 --> 00:29:25.340
<v David Boskovic>You'll learn every time, right?

471
00:29:25.440 --> 00:29:26.660
<v David Boskovic>Fire the exec.

472
00:29:26.940 --> 00:29:27.580
<v David Boskovic>Fire another one.

473
00:29:27.880 --> 00:29:29.220
<v David Boskovic>You'll learn each time.

474
00:29:29.760 --> 00:29:35.060
<v David Boskovic>Optimize for what helps you become the better person, better founder.

475
00:29:35.320 --> 00:29:38.300
<v David Boskovic>There are completely different on this point of execs.

476
00:29:38.300 --> 00:29:41.240
<v David Boskovic>They're completely different, very functional exec teams.

477
00:29:41.540 --> 00:29:42.860
<v David Boskovic>What's different about them?

478
00:29:43.100 --> 00:29:45.160
<v David Boskovic>The person who's leading them, right?

479
00:29:45.240 --> 00:29:48.840
<v David Boskovic>You build a team that orients around your strengths and your weaknesses,

480
00:29:48.960 --> 00:29:50.240
<v David Boskovic>and they make up for that.

